WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.650
Imagine Monday,
the third of January, twenty eleven.

2
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.050
At seven in the morning,
we issue a bike rental forecast.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.890
The target is the hour
from eight until nine.

4
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.770
The lead time is one hour
before that interval starts.

5
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.570
Our running example is
source record fifty four.

6
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.790
Its later recorded outcome is
one hundred fifty four rentals.

7
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.310
That number helps us check a
prediction after the interval finishes.

8
00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:33.300
The question throughout this tutorial is
what information the original forecast

9
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.450
could have used.

10
00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:42.110
The target hour is eight, the weekday
code is one, and the month code is one.

11
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:47.270
In this source, Sunday is zero,
so weekday one means Monday.

12
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.650
Holiday is zero and working day is one.

13
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.230
These calendar facts describe
the upcoming interval.

14
00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.270
The observed total is stored as cnt,
with value one hundred fifty four.

15
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.610
Calendar inputs describe the question.

16
00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.350
The later count supplies its answer.

17
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.410
Being stored beside each other
does not give them the same role.

18
00:01:11.458 --> 00:01:13.448
We keep three roles separate.

19
00:01:13.878 --> 00:01:18.408
The record identifier and timestamps
let us find and order an observation.

20
00:01:19.258 --> 00:01:22.148
The permitted calendar fields
become predictive inputs.

21
00:01:22.858 --> 00:01:27.028
The rental total becomes the separate
training or evaluation target.

22
00:01:27.878 --> 00:01:30.748
Metadata stays attached to
the row for traceability,

23
00:01:30.878 --> 00:01:32.648
outside the model feature vector.

24
00:01:33.258 --> 00:01:35.888
A later loader selects
named feature columns.

25
00:01:36.418 --> 00:01:41.078
Passing every numeric column into a model
would lose this distinction and could

26
00:01:41.078 --> 00:01:43.068
introduce information from the answer.

27
00:01:44.942 --> 00:01:47.012
Consider a different observation,

28
00:01:47.442 --> 00:01:50.432
the first row,
at midnight on the first of January.

29
00:01:51.142 --> 00:01:55.752
It records three casual rentals
and thirteen registered rentals.

30
00:01:56.302 --> 00:01:59.052
Their sum is sixteen,
which matches the total.

31
00:01:59.762 --> 00:02:03.032
Checking this equality
helps detect broken rows.

32
00:02:03.762 --> 00:02:08.662
Supplying those same hour components as
forecast inputs would reveal the target

33
00:02:08.662 --> 00:02:09.472
directly.

34
00:02:10.102 --> 00:02:15.012
We therefore validate the relationship and
exclude both components from prediction.

35
00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:20.742
A perfect score obtained from the answer's
components would not demonstrate a useful

36
00:02:20.742 --> 00:02:21.412
forecast.

37
00:02:23.603 --> 00:02:28.113
Historical files also contain weather
measured around the target hour.

38
00:02:28.463 --> 00:02:32.463
A measurement from eight to nine is
available at a different time from a

39
00:02:32.463 --> 00:02:34.393
forecast issued at seven.

40
00:02:34.823 --> 00:02:38.933
The initial task uses calendar
information available in advance.

41
00:02:39.543 --> 00:02:42.153
Observed weather remains
outside its inputs.

42
00:02:42.763 --> 00:02:47.523
A future weather experiment would need its
own availability contract and a forecast

43
00:02:47.523 --> 00:02:51.573
source or an explicitly
labelled historical comparison.

44
00:02:52.363 --> 00:02:56.413
A plausible looking feature can still
answer the wrong timing question.

45
00:02:58.563 --> 00:03:02.213
Let us deepen the contract
with the next hourly request.

46
00:03:02.983 --> 00:03:08.253
The original forecast is issued at seven
for the interval from eight until nine.

47
00:03:08.763 --> 00:03:14.183
Moving the requested interval to
nine until ten moves a one hour lead

48
00:03:14.183 --> 00:03:15.493
issue to eight.

49
00:03:16.303 --> 00:03:16.853
Monday,

50
00:03:17.643 --> 00:03:17.943
January,

51
00:03:18.443 --> 00:03:20.713
holiday and working day stay fixed.

52
00:03:21.523 --> 00:03:22.993
The requested hour changes.

53
00:03:23.763 --> 00:03:28.073
The old observed count of one hundred
fifty four belongs to the old interval.

54
00:03:28.423 --> 00:03:31.033
It cannot become the
answer to the new request.

55
00:03:33.316 --> 00:03:37.646
Here is a small probe using
the existing calendar encoder.

56
00:03:38.276 --> 00:03:41.486
The first mapping contains
the five declared inputs.

57
00:03:42.096 --> 00:03:44.526
The second replaces only the hour.

58
00:03:45.276 --> 00:03:48.326
Both calls produce forty
five calendar values.

59
00:03:49.076 --> 00:03:50.866
Neither call needs the rental total.

60
00:03:51.816 --> 00:03:55.886
Adding count to the mapping would
trigger the exact input field check.

61
00:03:56.256 --> 00:03:59.006
This makes an information
boundary executable.

62
00:03:59.776 --> 00:04:02.906
Metadata still retains
issue and target time,

63
00:04:03.116 --> 00:04:07.286
so a later prediction can be matched
to the request it actually answered.

64
00:04:09.441 --> 00:04:11.951
Now ask a changed condition question.

65
00:04:12.861 --> 00:04:13.751
At eight o’clock,

66
00:04:14.061 --> 00:04:18.031
can we use the observed total for
the interval from eight until nine?

67
00:04:18.621 --> 00:04:20.951
Pause and use the availability convention.

68
00:04:21.741 --> 00:04:24.931
Its label has started,
but the interval has not finished.

69
00:04:25.621 --> 00:04:29.731
The recorded total is therefore still
a later outcome under our convention.

70
00:04:30.461 --> 00:04:33.731
The key distinction is the time
information becomes available,

71
00:04:34.221 --> 00:04:38.371
rather than the position of its column
or whether the hour label is familiar.
